Claude receives two inputs per request: a system prompt and a user message.
The system prompt establishes the context — it tells Claude it is sizing work for a PHP-based educational platform, defines the five complexity buckets with their day ranges, instructs it to treat the story author as non-technical, and explicitly tells it to lower its confidence when historical comparators are sparse or old. This framing is what stops Claude from reasoning from general programming knowledge and anchors it to the calibration task.
The user message contains three things: the story text (summary and description, truncated to 1,000 characters), up to eight candidate files with their similarity scores, and the five most relevant historical issues. Each historical issue is presented with its MDL identifier, issue type, summary, complexity bucket, raw score, all five git signals, the number of matching files, and a recency label (recent / older / old).
Claude is asked to respond in structured JSON. The response contains a complexity bucket, optimistic/realistic/pessimistic day estimates, a confidence level, the single most similar historical issue and why, a rationale anchored to the comparators, a list of risk factors that could push the estimate higher, and a statement of what must be true for the optimistic case to hold. That last field is a deliberate forcing function — it requires Claude to make its assumptions explicit rather than produce a number without conditions.
Claude does not have access to the codebase itself. It reasons only from what is provided in each request.
